00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:29.860
<v James Spring>The two sides became so fixated on their hatred for each other that in a lot of videos, they don't even mention the seals. One morning, it's cold and foggy on the beach, and a 70-year-old pro-seal activist just has had enough of a shared use guy who's filming her and she charges right up to his camera.
<v James Spring>双方变得如此沉迷于对彼此的仇恨，以至于在许多视频中，他们甚至不提海豹。一天早晨，海滩上又冷又雾，一位70岁的支持海豹激进分子终于受够了一个正在拍她的共享利用派男士，她冲着他的相机冲了过去。

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:34.560
<v Man>So you're spitting on me now, huh? That's battery.
<v Man>所以你现在对我吐口水了，哈？那是人身攻击。

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:35.920
<v Woman>I didn't touch you.
<v Woman>我没碰你。

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:37.150
<v Man>You spit on me.
<v Man>你对我吐口水了。

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:38.050
<v Woman>You're harassing me.
<v Woman>你在骚扰我。

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:39.040
<v Man>You spit on me.
<v Man>你对我吐口水了。

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:42.880
<v Woman>I did. I spit on your camera.
<v Woman>我确实吐了。我吐在你的相机上了。

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:45.770
<v Man>No, you spit on me right in the forehead.
<v Man>不，你直接吐在我前额上了。

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:49.550
<v Woman>Poor boy.
<v Woman>可怜的男孩。

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:59.930
<v James Spring>At a certain point, the factions had been battling so long that they knew each other by name, knew where each other lived and worked. And the attacks were becoming more personal, the threats more vicious.
<v James Spring>到了某个时候，两派已经斗争了太久，以至于他们知道了对方的名字，知道对方住在哪儿、在哪儿工作。攻击变得更加个人化，威胁也更加恶毒。

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:03.720
<v Woman>Back away from us right now, you disgusting little turd.
<v Woman>现在就从我们身边退开，你这个恶心的脏东西。

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.570
<v Man>And you're a fucking bitch.
<v Man>你是个该死的婊子。

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:06.100
<v Woman>Piss off.
<v Woman>滚开。

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:08.180
<v Man>And somebody needs to fucking kill you.
<v Man>有人需要杀了你。

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:12.860
<v Woman>Yeah? Want to kill me? Why don't you give it a try? Why don't you give it a try?
<v Woman>是吗？想杀我？你为什么不试试？你为什么不试试？

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:14.780
<v Man>Don't let me catch you in an alley some time.
<v Man>别让我在小巷里逮到你。

00:21:14.780 --> 00:21:16.210
<v Woman>Oh, really? How about it?
<v Woman>哦，真的吗？怎么样？

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:26.600
<v James Spring>People on the shared use side had always accused the seal activists of valuing the lives of animals over people. And now, the sentiment began to ring more true. This one's a little hard to hear.
<v James Spring>共享利用派的人一直指责海豹激进分子视动物生命重于人命。现在，这种情绪变得更加真实了。这一段有点难听清。

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:30.460
<v Man>Don't get too close to the seals or I'll make your life miserable.
<v Man>别离海豹太近，否则我会让你的生活变得很痛苦。

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:34.190
<v James Spring>He said, "don't get too close to the seals or I'll make your life miserable."
<v James Spring>他说，“别离海豹太近，否则我会让你的生活变得很痛苦。”

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:35.420
<v Man>I'll bring my guns here.
<v Man>我会带枪来这里。

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:36.800
<v James Spring>"I'll bring my guns here."
<v James Spring>“我会带枪来这里。”

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:38.330
<v Man>I'll fuck you all up.
<v Man>我会干掉你们所有人。

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:59.780
<v James Spring>And "I'll fuck you all up." Throughout this war, the pro-seal camp had a unique weapon on their side-- the power of the federal government. And at some point, they really began to brandish it. The feds could arrest or issue citations to anyone who violates the Marine Mammal Protection Act. So the seal activists would film people as they harassed the seals or drove them from the beach.
<v James Spring>还有“我会干掉你们所有人”。在这场战争中，支持海豹阵营有一件独特的武器——联邦政府的力量。在某种程度上，他们真的开始挥舞这件武器。联邦当局可以逮捕或传唤任何违反《海洋哺乳动物保护法》的人。所以海豹激进分子会拍摄人们骚扰海豹或将它们赶出海滩的过程。

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:02.270
<v Woman>These are the lads that went for the swim.
<v Woman>这些是去游泳的家伙。

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:06.020
<v James Spring>And then they would keep the camera rolling and they'd get all shaky--
<v James Spring>然后他们会一直开着相机，变得非常抖动……

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:07.140
<v Man>Cover your license plate.
<v Man>拍下他的车牌。

00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:09.440
<v James Spring>--as they chased the lawbreakers all the way back to their car.
<v James Spring>……当他们追着违法者回到车上时。

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:10.670
<v Man>Cover your license plate.
<v Man>拍下他的车牌。

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:11.880
<v James Spring>--to record their license plate.
<v James Spring>……记录他们的车牌。

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:14.020
<v Woman>Oh, I got it.
<v Woman>哦，我拍到了。

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:31.270
<v James Spring>And then when they had it, the activists would turn over the video to the National Marine Fisheries Service and the feds would issue fines of $500 to anybody who made the seals flush. It was an incident like this one that brought the seal war to one of its darkest hours.
<v James Spring>拿到车牌后，激进分子会把视频交给国家海洋渔业局，联邦当局会向任何导致海豹受到惊吓的人处以500美元的罚款。正是这样的事件将海豹战争带向了它最黑暗的时刻之一。

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:33.100
<v Bryan Pease>You really want me to read this?
<v Bryan Pease>你真的想让我读这个？

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:33.930
<v James Spring>Read this all.
<v James Spring>把整个读出来。

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.160
<v Bryan Pease>I guess I was the first one who read it.
<v Bryan Pease>我想我是第一个读到它的。

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:54.630
<v James Spring>One day, Bryan Pease received an email that was addressed to one of his volunteers. It was a threat. The volunteer, a young woman who's identified only as "witness" in the federal court documents, had filmed the license plate of a pair of scuba divers who'd flushed some seals when they came up on the beach. Bryan read me the email.
<v James Spring>一天，布莱恩·皮斯收到了一封发给他其中一名志愿者的邮件。那是一个威胁。这名志愿者，在联邦法院文件中仅被称为“目击者”的年轻女子，拍摄了一对在海滩上驱赶海豹的水肺潜水员的车牌。布莱恩给我读了这封邮件。

00:22:54.630 --> 00:23:09.400
<v Bryan Pease>OK. "Two divers that used the Children's Pool in September have been cited by federal wildlife marshals for disturbing the seals. Witness was apparently the one who turned them in. The problem is, now, that revenge will be taken out on witness."
<v Bryan Pease>好的。“9月份在儿童泳池使用的两名潜水员已被联邦野生动物警长以骚扰海豹为由传唤。显然是目击者举报了他们。问题是，现在，报复将落在目击者身上。”

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:28.080
<v James Spring>The email continues, "Many of the local divers have close friends in the California motorcycle clubs. Motorcycle club members have been contacted about witness and she will be dealt with very harshly, if she is not killed. They know that if she's not in San Diego, she may be found in San Francisco.
<v James Spring>邮件继续写道，“当地许多潜水员与加州摩托车俱乐部有密切联系。摩托车俱乐部成员已被联络，目击者将受到严厉对付，如果不被杀死的话。他们知道，如果她不在圣地亚哥，她可能在旧金山被找到。”

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:45.140
<v James Spring>Either San Diego or Oakland hit hogs will be involved in her punishment. She's brought this on herself." And then it's signed "Biker Bobbie," with an I-E. Probably the least threatening Hell's Angels nickname of all time, but still.
<v James Spring>“圣地亚哥或奥克兰的恶棍将参与对她的惩罚。这是她自找的。”结尾署名是“Biker Bobbie”，拼写是I-E。可能是地狱天使有史以来最不具威胁性的绰号了，但依然如此。

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:49.040
<v James Spring>Was your coll-- was the woman who was threatened-- was she scared?
<v James Spring>你的同事……那个被威胁的女子……她害怕吗？

00:23:49.040 --> 00:24:05.490
<v Bryan Pease>Oh, she was terrified. Yeah. And I know her mother, who is also involved in the pro-seal protection campaign. And she had said that they were-- their whole family was scared for quite a while. So it was pretty serious.
<v Bryan Pease>哦，她吓坏了。是的。我认识她的母亲，她也参与了支持海豹保护运动。她说她们……全家人吓坏了好长一段时间。所以这相当严重。

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:19.470
<v James Spring>Biker Bobbie emailed two other threats to the Animal Protection and Rescue League. In one, he threatened to burn down the organization's storefront and to bomb a coffee shop. Bryan Pease turned the emails over to authorities.
<v James Spring>Biker Bobbie向动物保护与救援联盟发了两封威胁邮件。在其中一封里，他威胁要烧毁该组织的店面并炸掉一家咖啡馆。布莱恩·皮斯将这些邮件交给了当局。

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:44.370
<v James Spring>The female volunteer-- the one who was singled out with the death threat-- the one who'd sent the video of the two scuba divers to the National Marine Fisheries Service-- she was considered a federal witness. And threatening a federal witness is a federal crime so the FBI got involved. They tracked the email sender's IP address to a public library about five blocks away from the Children's Pool. The feds had Biker Bobbie in the crosshairs.
<v James Spring>那名女性志愿者——那个被死亡威胁单独列出来的——那个把两名潜水员的视频发送给国家海洋渔业局的人——她被视为联邦证人。威胁联邦证人是联邦犯罪，所以FBI介入了。他们通过IP地址追踪到距离儿童泳池五个街区外的一家公共图书馆。联邦当局锁定了Biker Bobbie。

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:55.930
<v Bryan Pease>Now, what he did was he went to the public library and he had an account called-- it was [email protected] . And then he would log in to his personal account two seconds later. So that's how they caught him.
<v Bryan Pease>他做的是去公共图书馆，他有一个账号叫……[email protected]。然后他会在两秒后登录他的个人账号。他们就是这样抓到他的。

00:24:55.930 --> 00:25:05.270
<v James Spring>And when they confronted the author of the emails, it turned out he was not an actual biker, nor was he a Bobbie. He was a scientist.
<v James Spring>当他们质问邮件作者时，发现他根本不是什么骑手，也不是什么Bobbie。他是一名科学家。

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:09.730
<v Kent Trego>Yeah, I sent them. Yeah. I sent them out of anger.
<v Kent Trego>是的，是我发的。是的。我是因为愤怒才发的。

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:12.660
<v James Spring>It was our marine science guy, Kent Trego.
<v James Spring>那就是我们的海洋科学派，肯特·特雷戈。

00:25:12.660 --> 00:25:28.900
<v Kent Trego>I regretted it. I regretted it after I did it. It just built up on me. And it was just one of those moments. But it was all words. There was never any-- never any intentions to hurt anybody.
<v Kent Trego>我很后悔。我做完之后就后悔了。这只是在我身上累积起来的。那只是那一刻的一时冲动。但那只是些话而已。从来没有……从来没有伤害任何人的意图。

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:46.260
<v James Spring>Kent remembers being at the Children's Pool on that day when the seal activist filmed the license plate of his two scuba diver pals. But he says he doesn't remember much else. He couldn't tell me any details from that day. Says he can't remember what he wrote in the emails. Even talking about it now-- it makes him squirm pretty good.
<v James Spring>肯特记得那天他在儿童泳池，当时海豹激进分子拍摄了他那两名水肺潜水员朋友的车牌。但他说是他不记得其他事情了。他无法告诉我那天细节。说他不记得他在邮件里写了什么。即使现在谈起，这也会让他感到相当不安。

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:49.630
<v Kent Trego>It wasn't meant to be a real threat or anything like that.
<v Kent Trego>它并不是真的威胁或类似的事情。

00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:57.390
<v James Spring>Obviously, the person receiving it on the other side would take it a little differently, I guess. The plan, I'm guessing, was just to scare them back-- to brush them back.
<v James Spring>显然，接收它的人会有不同的解读，我想。计划大概只是为了吓退他们，为了让他们撤回去。

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:08.780
<v Kent Trego>But I didn't even-- I didn't even-- as far as I recall, the content wasn't even meant to scare anybody. It was just more of a-- just a reaction.
<v Kent Trego>但我甚至没……甚至没……据我回忆，内容甚至不是为了吓唬谁。这更像是一种……只是一种反应。

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:10.590
<v James Spring>You were pretty direct about some stuff.
<v James Spring>你对某些事情相当直白。

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:21.500
<v Kent Trego>Yeah, like I said, I don't remember what I said. But I shouldn't have said it. But no, I didn't have any intention of hurting anybody. It's not a common sense reaction.
<v Kent Trego>是的，就像我说的那样，我不记得我说了什么。但我确实不应该说那些话。但没有，我没有任何伤害任何人的意图。这不是一种常识性的反应。

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:42.820
<v James Spring>Things started to come into sharper focus after he was arrested by federal agents as he walked back to his car from the beach. Kent pled guilty to the charge of threatening a federal witness. He served four and a half months in federal prison and then another six months of house arrest. He was banned from using a computer for a couple of years and ordered to stay away from the Children's Pool.
<v James Spring>在他从海滩回车的路上被联邦特工逮捕后，事情开始变得清晰起来。肯特对威胁联邦证人的指控认罪。他在联邦监狱服刑了四个月，随后又接受了六个月的居家监禁。他被禁止使用电脑几年，并被要求远离儿童泳池。

00:26:42.820 --> 00:26:52.010
<v James Spring>He took full responsibility for his actions. He apologized to the victim in court. He sent a letter of apology to the Animal Protection and Rescue League.
<v James Spring>他为自己的行为承担了全部责任。他在法庭上向受害者道歉。他向动物保护与救援联盟寄去了一封道歉信。

00:26:52.010 --> 00:27:04.290
<v James Spring>I tried to push Kent for details-- for an explanation-- just why he did it. Was there a chain of events? A final straw? He couldn't really give me a clear reason.
<v James Spring>我试图向肯特索要细节——一个解释——为什么他会这样做。是因为一连串事件吗？最后一根稻草吗？他真的没能给我一个明确的原因。

00:27:04.290 --> 00:27:12.850
<v Kent Trego>I guess, just wanted-- felt like you wanted to get even for some reason or whatever, or do something.
<v Kent Trego>我想，大概就是……感觉你想因为某种原因寻求报复，或者做点什么。

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:34.680
<v James Spring>I do know because I'm a human being. And really, who hasn't taken things a step or two further than maybe they should have? Like one time-- I've got this neighbor. He's a half block away from me. And on hot summer days, he'd blast his music really loud and it was always Alice in Chains, a band that I used to love before this.
<v James Spring>我知道，因为我也是人类。真的，谁没有把事情做得比可能应该做的更过分一步或两步呢？就像有一次……我有个邻居。他离我半个街区远。在炎热的夏日，他会把音乐开得震天响，而且总是放Alice in Chains，我曾经很喜欢的一支乐队。

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:46.100
<v James Spring>Anyway, my daughter Addie-- she was less than two-- 18 months. And she was a light sleeper. And it was already hard to get her down for naps. And then Alice in Chains would start rattling the neighborhood.
<v James Spring>总之，我女儿阿迪——她还不到两岁——18个月大。她睡眠很浅。让她午睡已经够难的了。然后Alice in Chains就开始震动整个社区。

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:55.720
<v James Spring>Addie starts crying, so I run to the neighbor's house and pound on the door. The guy doesn't answer so I call the cops. They show up and shut the neighbor down.
<v James Spring>阿迪开始哭，所以我跑到邻居家敲门。那家伙不开门，我就报警。他们到了并让邻居停下来。

00:27:55.720 --> 00:28:11.310
<v James Spring>The next day, same thing. It happened, like, six more times. Eventually, the cops stopped responding and nobody else seemed to care. And the sound blasting out of the neighbor's stereo made something come loose inside me.
<v James Spring>第二天，同样的事情又发生了。发生了大概六次。最终，警察不再响应，其他人似乎也不在乎。邻居音响里传出的声音让我内心深处有什么东西松动了。

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:25.440
<v James Spring>I went to my bedroom and unlocked my gun case and I loaded a revolver. And I walked out in my backyard to the corner closest to the music and bam, bam, bam, bam. I fired four shots into the dirt.
<v James Spring>我走到卧室，打开枪盒，给左轮手枪装上子弹。我走到后院最靠近音乐的角落，砰，砰，砰，砰。我对着泥土开了四枪。

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:36.160
<v James Spring>My wife is screaming at me and it hits me all at once. I'm the guy who's terrorizing the neighborhood. My wife didn't speak to me for a week.
<v James Spring>我妻子冲我大喊，我一下子清醒了。我成了那个恐吓社区的人。我妻子一周没跟我说话。

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:41.230
<v James Spring>So I get it. Everybody has their limits. All of us.
<v James Spring>所以我明白了。每个人都有底线。我们所有人。

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:42.760
<v James Spring>Tell me about the stun gun.
<v James Spring>跟我讲讲那个电击枪。

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:50.900
<v Bryan Pease>Well, that. Yeah. I'm happy to talk about that. I'm so sick of talking about it that I didn't bring it up. But you guys have turned over every rock in this.
<v Bryan Pease>好吧，那个。是的。我很乐意谈论那个。我谈得太恶心了，以至于我不想提。但你们已经把这里的一切都翻了个底朝天。

00:28:50.900 --> 00:29:12.280
<v James Spring>Yep. Even our animal-protecting, confrontation-avoiding attorney, Brian Pease. One of his fellow seal activists-- a woman-- had been raking seaweed one day at the Children's Pool, trying to create a mound or a barrier so she could tell visitors to stay behind it. And there was this 55-year-old shared use guy who was kicking away the seaweed line and just being a jerk.
<v James Spring>是的。甚至包括我们的动物保护、避免冲突的律师，布莱恩·皮斯。他的一位海豹激进分子同事——一位女性——有一天在儿童泳池耙海藻，试图制造一个土堆或屏障，好让游客待在它后面。有个55岁的共享利用派家伙在踢开海藻线，纯粹是在找茬。

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:22.280
<v Bryan Pease>She called me and told me that he's drunk. He's harassing the seals. He's harassing her. Can you do something? So I said, all right, I'm going to come down there. And I brought this thing for protection.
<v Bryan Pease>她打电话给我说他喝醉了。他在骚扰海豹。他在骚扰她。你能做点什么吗？所以我说，好吧，我要去那边。我带了这个东西以防万一。

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:30.400
<v James Spring>This thing, being the stun gun. Now, Brian went out of his way to explain that it wasn't technically a stun gun. This point was really important to him.
<v James Spring>这个东西，是电击枪。现在，布莱恩竭力解释说这在技术上不是电击枪。这一点对他来说非常重要。

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:41.110
<v James Spring>Says he had an expert witness declare it not a stun gun. But it says stun gun on the package and that's how the newspapers reported it. In any case, when he got down to the beach, the seaweed kicker confronted him.
<v James Spring>说他有一位专家证人宣布它不是电击枪。但包装上写着电击枪，报纸也是这么报道的。总之，他到了海滩时，那个踢海藻的人和他对质。

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:57.490
<v Bryan Pease>And then I'm standing near where this kelp line is. And he's just going back and forth from the kelp and then dumping it in the water. And then he comes back, picks up another two handfuls full of kelp and he goes, oh, you want some of this? And he just slings it right in my face-- just whips it right at me.
<v Bryan Pease>我站在海带线附近。他在海带线来回走动，然后把它扔进水里。然后他回来，抓起另外两把海带说，哦，你想要点这个吗？然后他直接把它甩在我脸上——直接朝我扔过来。

00:29:57.490 --> 00:30:17.200
<v Bryan Pease>That's when I pulled out the device, which I now know is not a stun gun, but it was a deterrent device. And I did try to-- I pushed him back with it. He admitted in the police report that he was not shocked through his thick sweatshirt.
<v Bryan Pease>就在那时我拿出了设备，我现在知道那不是电击枪，但它是一种威慑设备。我确实试着……我用它把他推开。他在警察报告中承认他并没有因为厚厚的运动衫而受到电击。

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:24.430
<v James Spring>You brought this device down there? You had a reason and then you felt emboldened enough to use it, right?
<v James Spring>你带了这个设备去那里？你有一个理由，然后你觉得足够有底气使用它，对吧？

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:38.180
<v Bryan Pease>From my perspective, I was enforcing the law. He's harassing seals. He's harassing people. And he attacked me and I was using reasonable force to defend myself.
<v Bryan Pease>从我的角度来看，我是在执行法律。他在骚扰海豹。他在骚扰人。他袭击了我，我是在使用合理的武力保护自己。

00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:41.230
<v James Spring>Anyway, the two men grappled and went to the ground.
<v James Spring>总之，两人扭打在一起倒在地上。

00:30:41.230 --> 00:30:44.380
<v Bryan Pease>Yeah. I think I pinned him down. I was saying, call the police.
<v Bryan Pease>是的。我想我把他压在下面了。我一直在说，报警。

00:30:44.380 --> 00:30:49.270
<v Bryan Pease>He was drunk. He was agitated. He was just being belligerent.
<v Bryan Pease>他喝醉了。他很激动。他只是在好战。

00:30:49.270 --> 00:31:01.990
<v Bryan Pease>But the stun-- yeah. I guess, when you ask what would I have done over again, I probably would have just pinned him down again. But the stun gun would not have been-- I call it a stun gun, but it's really not. This zapping device would not have been involved.
<v Bryan Pease>但电击……是的。我想，当你问我会重新做什么时，我可能只会再次把他压住。但电击枪就不会被……我叫它电击枪，但它真的不是。这个电击设备就不会被卷入其中。

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:17.920
<v James Spring>Bryan was the one who was arrested. He pled no contest to assault with a stun gun. Bryan's a smart guy and super thoughtful. But I think he got caught up in his herd's mentality and people here forgot that there are more options than just fight or flight.
<v James Spring>布莱恩是被逮捕的人。他承认了使用电击枪袭击的指控。布莱恩很聪明，非常体贴。但我认为他陷入了这种群体心态，这里的人忘了选择不止是战斗或逃跑。

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:23.220
<v Bryan Pease>They went right to their reptilian brain and their prefrontal cortex got shut off there. Is that you're saying?
<v Bryan Pease>他们直接进入了爬行动物大脑模式，前额叶皮层在那儿关闭了？你是这个意思吗？

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:35.050
<v James Spring>Yeah. It feels that way. And for me, personally, somebody gets in my face, I don't think about the legal parts of it or whatever. I react from the animal part of me, right?
<v James Spring>是的。感觉就是这样。对我个人来说，有人凑到我面前，我不会考虑其中的法律部分或其他什么。我从我的动物本能出发，对吧？

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:41.200
<v Bryan Pease>That's what I did. And that's why I didn't want to go there anymore. It's almost like asking for trouble. Why do it? Why go there?
<v Bryan Pease>我就是这么做的。这就是我为什么不想再去那儿的原因。简直就像是在自找麻烦。为什么要这样做？为什么要再去？

00:31:41.200 --> 00:31:47.330
<v Kent Trego>The appropriate thing would be just to walk away from it and just be gone.
<v Kent Trego>合适的做法是直接走开，然后离开。

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:51.270
<v James Spring>This is Kent again. And on this point, both men agree.
<v James Spring>这是肯特，又是肯特。在这一点上，两人都同意。

00:31:51.270 --> 00:32:08.920
<v Kent Trego>And that's what I should have done if I had been smart about it. I think that's what people need to do in conflicting situations-- walk away from them. I'll never be involved in anything like this again. Period.
<v Kent Trego>那才是我如果聪明的话应该做的。我认为人们在冲突的情况下需要做的是——走开。我再也不会参与任何这样的事情了。句号。

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:27.400
<v James Spring>In the summer of 2014, the California Coastal Commission voted to completely close the beach at Children's Pool to humans during pupping season, from December 15 to May 15. Bryan and Kent both support this decision. Anybody who wants to see the seals can still watch them from the sidewalk and the seawall.
<v James Spring>2014年夏天，加州海岸委员会投票决定在产仔季节，即12月15日至5月15日，完全禁止人类进入儿童泳池海滩。布莱恩和肯特都支持这一决定。任何想看海豹的人仍然可以从人行道和防波堤上观看。

00:32:27.400 --> 00:32:32.950
<v James Spring>And it works. People aren't screaming at each other. Seals aren't being flushed from the beach.
<v James Spring>而且它有效。人们不再互相尖叫。海豹不再被从海滩上赶走。

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:58.500
<v James Spring>During the rest of the year, there's a permanent guideline rope down on the sand to provide a buffer between humans and harbor seals. It's not perfect, but it's a pretty good compromise. Some of the seal activists are still patrolling the beach during the seven months when it's partially opened to the public. And some of the shared use people are mounting a legal campaign to have the Coastal Commission decision overturned. But mostly, it's just nice now.
<v James Spring>在这一年的其余时间里，沙滩上有一根永久性的引导绳，在人类和港海豹之间提供缓冲。它并不完美，但这是一个相当好的妥协。一些海豹激进分子在向公众部分开放的七个月里仍然在海滩上巡逻。一些共享利用派的人正在开展法律行动，要求撤销海岸委员会的决定。但最重要的是，现在这里感觉不错。

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:01.960
<v Girl>Daddy, look, I see one! Right there! Right there.
<v Girl>爸爸，看，我看到一只！就在那儿！就在那儿。

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:03.310
<v Father>Oh, yeah.
<v Father>哦，是的。

00:33:03.310 --> 00:33:13.810
<v James Spring>The thing that you don't hear in all those YouTube videos is the sound of the place without the hate, when people are not screaming. When the sun is shining and the seals are sprawled out across the white sand.
<v James Spring>你在那些YouTube视频中听不到的是，当没有仇恨、人们不再尖叫时，那个地方的声音。当阳光普照，海豹横卧在白沙滩上时。

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:15.880
<v Girl>Look, he's rolling onto his stomach.
<v Girl>看，它正在翻身趴在肚皮上。

00:33:15.880 --> 00:33:28.680
<v James Spring>Sometimes you see a mother nursing her pups. It feels like you're being let in on a secret, like this is how the world is without us. We, as a species, are aggressive.
<v James Spring>有时你会看到母亲在哺育幼崽。这感觉就像你被允许分享一个秘密，就像这就是没有我们时世界本来的样子。作为一个物种，我们是有侵略性的。

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:40.450
<v James Spring>We're predatory, territorial. We're pretty selfish. And here at the Children's Pool, we've got the place surrounded. The seals keep coming back. Clearly, they like it here.
<v James Spring>我们是掠食性的，领地意识强。我们很自私。在这里的儿童泳池，我们把这个地方包围了。海豹继续回来。显然，它们喜欢这里。

00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:42.880
<v Father>Here comes another one up there on that little island.
<v Father>那边的那个小岛上又上来了一只。

00:33:42.880 --> 00:34:03.800
<v James Spring>And even though I don't have any evidence or scientific data to prove it, I still think that the seals totally dig us, despite our natures.
<v James Spring>尽管我没有任何证据或科学数据来证明这一点，但我依然认为海豹完全喜欢我们，尽管我们的本性如此。

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:43.550
<v Ira Glass>James Spring in San Diego. Since we first aired this story a year ago, a group of the shared use advocates sued the city of San Diego to get the beach opened up again year round to humans. A judge ruled in their favor last May. The city appealed that decision and was granted the right to close the beach this winter while the court case plays out.
<v Ira Glass>詹姆斯·斯普林在圣地亚哥。自从我们一年前首次播出这个故事以来，一群共享利用拥护者起诉圣地亚哥市，要求海滩全年向人类开放。法官去年五月作出了有利于他们的裁决。市政府对该决定提出了上诉，并被准许在诉讼期间在今年冬天关闭海滩。

00:34:43.550 --> 00:35:00.230
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, the most successful animal outlaw the range country out west every knew. And by that, I mean the animal itself was seen as a criminal. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，西部草原上曾经知道的最成功的动物亡命之徒。我的意思是，动物本身被视为罪犯。一分钟后，从芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:12.000
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "When the Beasts Come Marching In." We have stories of what happens when animals intrude on the world that we humans have constructed for ourselves. We've arrived at Act Two of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，“当猛兽来袭”。我们有关于当动物闯入我们人类为自己建造的世界时会发生什么的故事。我们已经进入节目的第二幕。

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:34.790
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "Hungry Like a US Department of Agriculture Contract Employee." OK. Back in Act One of our show, we heard about modern people trying to accommodate the wild animals that just show up in their midst. But of course, back in the day, generally, a wild animal showed up, we would just kill it. Take, for example, this press release that the federal government put out nearly 100 years ago.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“像美国农业部合同员工一样饥饿”。好的。在节目的第一幕，我们听到了现代人试图适应出现在他们中间的野生动物。但当然，在过去，通常当野生动物出现时，我们只会杀死它。以联邦政府近100年前发布的一份新闻稿为例。

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:44.260
<v Michael Chernus>"US department of Agriculture press release. Monday, January 17, 1921. The Custer Wolf is dead.
<v Michael Chernus>“美国农业部新闻稿。1921年1月17日，星期一。卡斯特狼死了。

00:35:44.260 --> 00:36:04.640
<v Michael Chernus>He was the master criminal of the animal world. For nine years, this wolf had lived as an outlaw. The cruelest, the most sagacious, the most successful animal outlaw that the range country had ever known. His cruelty was surpassed only by his cunning. Here tonight, tomorrow night he'd devastated a range half a hundred miles away."
<v Michael Chernus>它是动物界的犯罪大师。九年来，这只狼一直作为亡命之徒生活。这是草原上所知的最残忍、最睿智、最成功的动物亡命之徒。它的残忍仅次于它的狡猾。今晚在这里，明晚它就在五十英里外的草原上肆虐。”

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:18.050
<v Ira Glass>Yes, this is an actual press release from the federal government about the government hiring a guy to kill a wolf. Actor Michael Chernus read it for us. And OK, some context. Wolves used to be common in the mountains out west.
<v Ira Glass>是的，这是一份来自联邦政府关于政府雇人杀狼的真实新闻稿。演员迈克尔·切纳斯为我们朗读了它。好的，提供一些背景。狼曾经在西部的山脉中很常见。

00:36:18.050 --> 00:36:31.450
<v Ira Glass>But when settlers arrived, we killed off the animals that wolves used to feed on-- bison, elk, deer. And so the wolves turned to livestock-- our livestock-- for food. We were not into that at all.
<v Ira Glass>但当定居者到达后，我们杀死了狼曾经赖以生存的动物——野牛、麋鹿、鹿。所以狼转向了牲畜——我们的牲畜——来寻找食物。我们一点也不喜欢那样。

00:36:31.450 --> 00:36:57.380
<v Ira Glass>So ranchers and the federal government set out to exterminate the wolves. Between 1883 and 1930, more than 80,000 wolves were killed for bounties in Montana alone. And the governor wanted everybody to know what a good job they were doing for the taxpayers. And so they put out press releases that include facts, but do not seem constrained by facts. Though it does seem true that this wolf was really good at being a wolf.
<v Ira Glass>所以牧场主和联邦政府着手灭绝狼。在1883年至1930年间，仅在蒙大拿州，就有超过8万只狼因悬赏而被杀。州长希望每个人都知道他们为纳税人做了多么好的工作。所以他们发布了新闻稿，其中包括事实，但似乎并不受事实限制。虽然这只狼确实非常擅长做狼，这似乎是真的。

00:36:57.380 --> 00:37:08.610
<v Michael Chernus>"He loped through every kind of danger and spurned them all. He sniffed at the subtlest poison and passed it by. When he killed for food, he took only the choicest animals.
<v Michael Chernus>“它跳跃着穿过各种危险，并对它们不屑一顾。它嗅出最细微的毒药并绕道而行。当它捕食时，它只选择最上等的动物。

00:37:08.610 --> 00:37:36.760
<v Michael Chernus>But sometimes, he killed in atrocious ways for the mere sake of killing. Often he wounded cattle, breaking their legs, biting off their tails, mutilating them in unspeakable ways. And there was no man throughout that region who did not feel a shiver run down between his shoulder blades when, alone or in the dark, he thought of this gray devil of the desert.
<v Michael Chernus>但有时，它仅仅为了杀戮而以残暴的方式杀害动物。它经常伤害牛，折断它们的腿，咬掉它们的尾巴，以无法形容的方式残害它们。在该地区，没有一个人在独自一人或在黑暗中想到这只沙漠灰魔鬼时，脊背不感到寒意。

00:37:36.760 --> 00:37:46.260
<v Michael Chernus>Four years ago, his mate was killed. He never took another. And many people supposed that he devoted himself to revenge for her death.
<v Michael Chernus>四年前，它的配偶被杀了。它再也没有找过别的配偶。许多人认为它致力于为她的死复仇。

00:37:46.260 --> 00:38:09.410
<v Michael Chernus>Later on, he attached to himself two coyotes-- not his equals, but his servants. He never permitted them to come near him and they could feed from his kills only after he himself had finished. They traveled far out on his flanks, giving him warning of ambush or approaching danger, and adding to the atmosphere of mystery that surrounded him."
<v Michael Chernus>后来，它给自己招募了两只土狼——不是它的同类，而是它的仆人。它从不允许它们靠近自己，只有在它自己吃完后，它们才能吃它杀死的猎物。它们在它的侧翼远行，给它提供伏击或即将到来的危险预警，并增加了围绕着它的神秘氛围。”

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:18.740
<v Ira Glass>OK. I'm just going to jump in here again. This is the federal government sending this out as a press release. And it says they took nine years to capture this wolf.
<v Ira Glass>好的。我又要插话了。这是联邦政府作为新闻稿发出的。它说他们花了九年时间才抓住这只狼。

00:38:18.740 --> 00:38:28.910
<v Ira Glass>The wolf operated around Custer, South Dakota. And the fact that they gave him a name-- "The Custer Wolf"-- that was something they did with a lot of notorious wolves back then. So how did it take nine years?
<v Ira Glass>狼在南达科他州的卡斯特周围活动。他们给它起了一个名字——“卡斯特狼”——那是当时他们对许多臭名昭著的狼所做的事情。那为什么花了九年？

00:38:28.910 --> 00:38:42.730
<v Ira Glass>Well, the press release explains the bounty started at $100. And trappers tried, sportsmen tried. They used poison, they used dogs. Nobody could kill this wolf. They raised the bounty higher and higher, year after year, till it was $500.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，新闻稿解释说悬赏从100美元开始。捕兽者试过，运动员试过。他们用过毒药，用过狗。没有人能杀死这只狼。他们一年又一年地提高赏金，直到500美元。

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:59.040
<v Ira Glass>And eight years in, in March of 1920, USDA's Bureau of Biological Survey went to a hunter named HP Williams, who I'm picturing-- actually, I'm picturing the shark hunter in the movie Jaws, though I have no facts at all to support that.
<v Ira Glass>第八年，1920年3月，美国农业部生物调查局找到了一个名叫惠普·威廉姆斯的猎人，我在脑海中勾勒他的形象——其实，我勾勒的是电影《大白鲨》里的鲨鱼猎人，虽然我没有任何事实依据支持这一点。

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:08.710
<v Ira Glass>The government goes to him with instructions to stay with the wolf until the wolf is killed no matter how much time it takes. Williams went.
